{
  "domains": [
    {
      "path": "",
      "display_name": "theLook e-commerce",
      "description": "Synthetic online retailer. Orders, items, products, inventory, fulfillment, users, and web events.",
      "context_md": "theLook is a synthetic online retailer: registered users place orders, each order contains one or more order items, items map to inventory and products, and products ship from distribution centers. Web events capture browsing.\n\nGlobal rules:\n- **Currency is USD.** All money columns (`SALE_PRICE`, `COST`, `RETAIL_PRICE`) are US dollars.\n- **Revenue** is the sum of `ORDER_ITEMS.SALE_PRICE` for items not cancelled and not returned. Cancelled and returned items do not count.\n- A **completed order** is an order whose `STATUS = 'Complete'`.\n- A **customer** is a user who has placed at least one order. A user who only browsed is not a customer.\n- Identity: users are keyed by `USERS.ID` (= `ORDERS.USER_ID` = `ORDER_ITEMS.USER_ID`).\n- **Return rate** is item-grain: returned items divided by all items, where an item is returned when `ORDER_ITEMS.RETURNED_AT` is not null (equivalently item `STATUS = 'Returned'`). Only use order-level returns when a question explicitly asks about orders.\n- Products are identified by `PRODUCTS.ID`; product names are not unique. Include the product id when reporting per-product results.\n- `USERS.STATE` is populated for users of every country; questions 'by state' cover all countries unless the question restricts to one.\n- Grain trap: revenue lives at the order-item level. Count orders with `COUNT(DISTINCT ORDER_ID)`, never `COUNT(*)` over order items.\n\nDomains: [sales](sales), [catalog](catalog), [fulfillment](fulfillment), [users](users), [web](web)."
    },
    {
      "path": "sales",
      "display_name": "Sales",
      "description": "Orders and order items: the transactional core.",
      "context_md": "Orders and the items inside them.\n\n- `ORDERS`: one row per order. `STATUS` moves through Processing, Shipped, Complete, Cancelled, Returned. `RETURNED_AT` is set when the order came back.\n- `ORDER_ITEMS`: one row per item sold; `SALE_PRICE` is the per-item revenue. This is the grain for revenue.\n\nChild: [measuring sales](sales/measuring)."
    },
    {
      "path": "sales/measuring",
      "display_name": "Measuring sales",
      "description": "How to turn orders and items into revenue, AOV, and returns.",
      "context_md": "Definitions used across the business:\n- **Revenue**: `SUM(SALE_PRICE)` over order items not cancelled and not returned.\n- **Average order value**: revenue divided by distinct orders.\n- **Return rate**: share of orders with a non-null `RETURNED_AT`.\n- **Total spend**: a customer's lifetime revenue (revenue grouped by user)."
    },
    {
      "path": "catalog",
      "display_name": "Catalog",
      "description": "Products and the physical inventory units sold.",
      "context_md": "- `PRODUCTS`: the catalog. `RETAIL_PRICE` is list price, `COST` is unit cost; margin is the difference.\n- `INVENTORY_ITEMS`: individual physical units. Each order item is fulfilled by one inventory item. `SOLD_AT` marks when a unit was sold."
    },
    {
      "path": "fulfillment",
      "display_name": "Fulfillment",
      "description": "Distribution centers and shipping timeline.",
      "context_md": "- `DISTRIBUTION_CENTERS`: the warehouses products ship from (with lat/long).\n- Shipping timeline lives on `ORDERS`/`ORDER_ITEMS`: `CREATED_AT` -> `SHIPPED_AT` -> `DELIVERED_AT`, and `RETURNED_AT` for returns."
    },
    {
      "path": "users",
      "display_name": "Users",
      "description": "The registered user base; customers are users who ordered.",
      "context_md": "`USERS`: one row per registered user, with demographics, location, and `TRAFFIC_SOURCE` (how they were acquired). A user becomes a **customer** once they place an order."
    },
    {
      "path": "web",
      "display_name": "Web events",
      "description": "Clickstream: sessions, page views, and traffic sources.",
      "context_md": "`EVENTS`: one row per web event, grouped into sessions by `SESSION_ID` and ordered by `SEQUENCE_NUMBER`. `EVENT_TYPE` and `URI` describe the action. Note: the events table is large and is not bundled in this repo's `data/`; load it from the BigQuery public dataset if needed."
    }
  ],
